lerna-debug.log*

node_modules
dist
dist-ssr
coverage
*.local

# This project is pnpm only. A stray npm lockfile makes tooling pick the wrong
# package manager, so it is ignored rather than committed.
package-lock.json

# `pnpm sagis:harvest` writes two kinds of thing, and only one is ignored.
#
# data/sagis/parcels.ndjson is 11MB of full records with geometry, written in
# the shape that loads into Postgres. It is rebuilt from the county rather than
# edited, so it stays out of git.
#
# public/parcels/ is the 13MB the browser actually fetches, and it IS committed,
# because the deployed site has no other way to get it: it is served as static
# files and there is no database behind it yet. Without it the deploy falls back
# to the 48-lot demo fixture. This goes away when Supabase does the job.
#
# The pattern is anchored with a leading slash on purpose. A bare `data/`
# matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, so it also swallowed
# src/lib/data/, and three source files written after that rule was added were
# silently never committed. Every deploy built from the repository failed on the
# missing module while it built fine on this machine.
/data/

# Environment. Never commit real credentials, .env.example carries placeholders.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.production

# Supabase CLI local state
supabase/.temp
supabase/.branches

